{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Independent exact-head review of 106259ea8a5b44318a80a233460cba25430c2879 on request/github-observation-authorship, under review request c24a7407 and promise 5e7dce01, co-signing both artifacts: e7f4300a at internal/connector for parent request 1214a363 and ac3403bd at cmd/gitseq-github for the signing-identity child f17681bc.\n\nI wrote both governing requests, so I held this to the conditions as written rather than to the fix I had in mind, and I re-ran every piece of evidence rather than reading the recorded version of it. Where the implementation chose differently from my sketch I say whether the choice is better, not whether it matches.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD IS. Four files, +210/-27, cut from current main 4200aa27. Fold takes a connectorActor fingerprint and skips any statement whose Actor is not it. Statement gains an Actor field, and the CLI adapter carries the projected statement's Actor across. loadObservationIdentity resolves the address, loads the key once, derives the fingerprint from the loaded key, refuses on mismatch with the configured one, and returns name, fingerprint and private key together; that one value then feeds both the Fold guard and the signing call.\n\nTHE CRUX, RE-VERIFIED. A guard keyed on authorship is worth nothing if the forger supplies the authorship. It does not: internal/app/app.go:1401 builds the workroom Record with Actor: intent.ActorFingerprint(event.Signed.ActorKey), derived from the signing key on the verified kernel event, and internal/workroom/fold.go:1608 copies it into the projected Statement unchanged, with fold.go:332 refusing any record with an empty Actor. So the field this guard reads is the verified signer.\n\nTHE SECOND CRUX, WHICH IS THE CHILD REQUEST AND THE PART I CARED ABOUT MOST. The reason f17681bc existed is that a fingerprint from configuration and a key from disk are two answers to one name, and if they disagree the guard filters on an identity that never signs anything — the correspondence is permanently empty, every issue is re-observed, and \"a foreign statement cannot suppress a genuine observation\" becomes vacuously true because nothing matches at all. That failure looks exactly like a working guard. This head closes it structurally rather than by checking: observationIdentity carries the name, the derived fingerprint and the private key as one value, so filtering and signing cannot be given different identities without changing the type. The equality check is then a refusal at construction, not a condition someone must remember at each call site. That is better than what I asked for, which was only that the two agree.\n\nORDERING, WHICH THE CONDITION MADE PART OF THE PROPERTY AND WHICH I CHECKED IN THE SOURCE RATHER THAN IN THE TEST. loadObservationIdentity is called at main.go:105, and the first outbound call is github.NewClient followed by github.Fetch at :138-139. So a mismatched connector refuses before it has touched GitHub at all, which is what I asked for: the run fails at the identity boundary rather than after acting under an identity it cannot vouch for. It also sits after the --propose early return, so the regression that caused the previous head to be replaced is not reintroduced by the fix for it.\n\nALL THREE MUTATIONS RE-RUN BY ME, EACH FAILING ITS OWN NAMED TEST. Deleting the Fold guard fails TestForeignStatementCannotSuppressTheConnectorObservation with \"foreign statement suppressed the genuine observation\", and separately fails TestObservedStatementsPreserveAuthorshipForTheCorrespondenceFold, so the library and the adapter seam are pinned independently. Deleting the fingerprint equality check fails TestObservationIdentityRefusesConfiguredFingerprintMismatch with \"mismatched connector identity error = \u003cnil\u003e\". Reintroducing the premature identity lookup ahead of the propose branch fails TestDryRunProposalDoesNotRequireLocalConnectorCustody with \"dry-run proposal required local connector custody: unknown actor address\". That last one is worth saying plainly: the test reproduces the exact regression that cost this lane a head, in the words an operator would have seen, so the mistake cannot be made twice silently.\n\nTHE REST OF THE CONDITIONS. Foreign suppression is closed, proven above. First-writer ordering between genuine observations is unchanged: the guard is an early continue before the source and external_id checks, and the \"if not exists\" record at the bottom of the loop is untouched. Selector canonicalization is covered by TestObservationIdentityCanonicalizesActorAddresses, and the observe path now passes connector.Name rather than the raw argument, so name, @name and fingerprint reach charterIsLive as one value. The connector library remains pure: Fold gained a parameter, not a field, and opens nothing.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head with a clean worktree. gofmt -l over cmd, internal, host and spike: no output. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. make vet: clean. make build: both binaries. go test ./... -count=1: 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL, internal/apphost has no test files.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layers are Application connector — internal/connector/github and cmd/gitseq-github, which docs/reference/architecture.md describes as applying Workroom charters and emitting Workroom observations, replaceable and outside the kernel. Both contracts are preserved. Identity resolution sits on the correct side of the seam: the command turns an address into a loaded key and a fingerprint, and hands the library a plain string. The library learned that one of its inputs has an author; it did not learn about actors, custody or configuration, so it stays replaceable. internal/app, internal/workroom and internal/kernel are untouched. No contract changed, so no update to that page is required at this head and none was made.\n\nSECURITY. Actor resolution: ResolveActorAddress refuses an unknown address, and the derived-versus-configured check refuses a key that is not the one configuration names, both before any network call. Forged projected statements: the guard reads the verified signer, so forging authorship requires the connector's key, at which point the forger is the connector. Identity trust: unchanged in kind — the connector trusts the repository's own configuration and custody, the same source that decides which key it signs with. Empty or unknown actor: covered by the resolution error; and every projected statement carries a non-empty signer, so an empty fingerprint could only fail closed. Duplicate ordering: unchanged. Failure modes all fall toward refusing or re-observing rather than toward silently observing nothing, which is the correct direction. Private state: none added. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The observationIdentity struct is the smallest thing that makes the property structural instead of remembered, and collapsing two lookups into one load removes a line rather than adding a layer.\n\nTwo observations recorded rather than requested. First, the propose path still passes the raw actorName to charterIsLive while the observe path passes the canonical connector.Name, and charterBinds compares body[\"actor\"] by string equality, so --actor @name canonicalizes for one operation and not the other. It fails closed, and it is not a regression — both paths used the raw name before this head — but it is a new asymmetry, and the natural place to fix it is whenever the propose path next needs an identity for its own reasons. Second, loadObservationIdentity type-asserts private.Public().(ed25519.PublicKey) without checking, which would panic rather than error on a non-ed25519 key; that matches the existing idiom at internal/app/app.go:429, so changing it here alone would make the codebase less consistent rather than more careful.\n\nMergeable. The head is pushed and cut from current main; both artifacts are live, ordinarily stale, and neither describes a superseded world; the obsolete 311a940f artifacts and review request 3c84f1f9 are retired, so nothing here collides with the abandoned head; and this approval names both artifacts, so a receipt may retire each path's predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e7f4300a665397b28f328be736b2531256ec3623","head":"106259ea8a5b44318a80a233460cba25430c2879","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:236ff0752bf1fa3ddbdffb86a4f43bb10bd28efe, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:24b10235ba0b17619ee94a7982888bb4406f053a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c1dbd1fc3202394ea1d9b7a8d430b68d51f3730e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c6ba7ead37cc4c54dd16aeb75a1b7c3c0f4ddc58 and 1 more","verdict":"approved"}}